WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.140
Today’s episode of Two Cents is all about
the ladies.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.140
Oh.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.140
Should I go?

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:07.140
No.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.240
You should listen.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.340
Okay ladies.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.570
Time for a little real talk.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:24.610
Women report feeling high or overwhelming
levels of financial stress twice as often

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:25.610
as men.

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:26.610
Why is that?

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.130
There are two major reasons:

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.980
The first is compensation.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.550
The gender pay gap is real and it is nasty.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:40.510
In 1988, American women made an average of
66% of what a man would make in an equivalent

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:41.510
position.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.700
And today, that number
is up to…

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.579
80%.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:52.350
And during that same period, women have had
to shoulder more financial burden, with dual

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:57.359
income households on the rise, and 42% of
today’s mothers with children under 18 being

00:00:57.359 --> 00:00:59.920
the primary or sole breadwinner.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.129
And this has life-long implications.

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:06.250
The less you’re paid, the less you’re
contributing to social security, which means

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:10.650
we have to be setting aside MORE of our already
smaller paychecks for retirement.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:15.361
And that doesn’t even take into account
the fact that we tend to live longer, which

00:01:15.361 --> 00:01:19.910
is hardly a plus if you’re spending the
those extra few years in abject poverty.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:23.770
The second reason is confidence, or a lack
thereof.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:28.400
In my research and professional experience,
I have seen women consistently undervalue

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.060
their money management skills--even when they
tend to make the right decisions!

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.200
In a University of Cambridge study, just 42%
of female business owners said their business

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:45.030
is prospering compared to 62% of males--despite
the fact that the female-run businesses reported

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.170
higher average profits!

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:52.340
Less than half of women said they’d be comfortable
consulting a financial professional, and only

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.530
20% said they discuss money with friends and
family.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:59.010
That doesn’t leave a lot of opportunities
to increase your skill set.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:04.310
Shrinking that wage gap is a long-term, collective
battle, but you have more personal control

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:09.399
over your financial confidence, and it starts
with learning why women are actually GREAT

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:10.590
with money.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.890
Women are better investors!

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:18.030
Only 43% of women said they were confident
in their investments, but a 2014 study showed

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:23.220
that women outpaced equivalent male investors'
returns by 12%!

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.420
Why?

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.010
Because women tend to be more conservative
investors and think of their financial picture

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.010
more holistically.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.940
Men, on the other hand, are more focused on
achieving certain rates of return, which drives

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:40.320
them to buy and sell more often and be more
likely to hop on trendy bandwagons like Tesla

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:41.330
and Bitcoin!

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:47.020
This impatience means that men are 25% more
likely to lose money on their investments.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:50.840
And the same thing seems to be true in the
bond market!

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:56.240
During the past 15 years, women run fixed-income
funds outperformed the average returns of

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.310
those of run by men.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:00.430
Women manage debt better!

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:05.230
Although we typically earn less, there is
evidence we are better at managing personal

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.230
debt.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.819
Back in 2015 Experian came out with a study
that showed on average, women had slightly

00:03:10.819 --> 00:03:12.640
higher credit scores than men.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.240
And men tended to have more consumer debt
on the books.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:22.490
According to one study in the developing world,
women had a 97% payback rate on micro-loans

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:27.490
extended to them and were far more likely
to reinvest back into their businesses rather

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:30.600
than blow it on luxuries like men tend to.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.930
Women make excellent business owners!

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:36.069
- Women now account for 40% of new entrepreneurs
in the United States.

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:38.320
And many of them are killing it!

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.069
According to a Harvard Business review study,
women tend to excel at influencing others

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:48.440
to support their vision, and managing operations
behind the scenes, especially when resources

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:49.440
are limited.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.960
We’re pretty used to having to make the
most of what we’ve got.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.880
And we are gaining ground!

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.250
Approximately 40% of U.S. working women now
out-earn their husbands.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:01.220
And over the next decade, women will control
two-thirds

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:04.160
of all consumer wealth in the United States,

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:09.350
with many female baby boomers experiencing
a double inheritance, from parents and their

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:10.350
spouse.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:11.930
We’re also getting more educated!

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:19.829
In 2013, women earned 56% of all bachelor’s
degrees and 60% of all master’s degrees.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:25.870
And we hold the keys to the greatest economic
tool on earth: 75% of women reported that

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.770
they were the primary shoppers for their households.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:33.940
Which means we are making the decisions that
determine which products and services prosper.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.710
Obviously, this is a topic I'm passionate
about and a short video simply can’t cover

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.490
all the problems and possibilities women face.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:47.710
But if I can leave you with one thing it’s
this…financial empowerment will NOT be given

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:48.710
to you.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:50.500
It must be claimed.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:51.500
And that’s--

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.050
So let’s stretch out our hands ladies!

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.919
Do it for yourself and the ones closest to
you.

00:04:55.919 --> 00:04:59.770
They’re following your lead whether you
realize it or not!

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:01.220
And that’s our--

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.250
And that is my ONE cent!

